---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Cell from '../../components/arch/Cell.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// Big-symbol grid: 5 reels across, 3 cells down, 68-unit cells, 6-unit gap.
const CS = 68;
const GAP = 6;
const GX0 = 60;
const GY0 = 90;
const cx = (reel: number) => GX0 + reel * (CS + GAP);
const cy = (cell: number) => GY0 + cell * (CS + GAP);
const inBlock = (r: number, c: number) => r >= 1 && r <= 2 && c >= 0 && c <= 1;
---
<Architecture
  title="Symbols and pooling"
  subtitle="The ReelSymbol lifecycle, the registry, the factory, the pool."
  eyebrow="Cells"
>
  <p>
    A spinning reel is hungry for symbols. Every time the strip scrolls one
    cell past the edge, the symbol that left comes back at the other end
    wearing a new identity, and over a five-second spin that happens hundreds
    of times across five reels. If each of those moments created a new sprite
    and dropped the old one, the garbage collector would eventually pause the
    game mid-spin, and the player would see the reels stutter. A Hold &amp;
    Win board makes it worse: hundreds of cells, each with its own art and
    maybe its own animation runtime.
  </p>

  <p>
    So pixi-reels treats a symbol as a reusable actor rather than a
    disposable picture. A <code>ReelSymbol</code> is the object that draws
    one cell. Instances are borrowed from a pool, given an identity (the
    symbol id, like <code>'cherry'</code>), shown, taken back, and lent out
    again. The symbol itself knows nothing about its weight, its size in
    cells or its draw order; that metadata lives in a separate record, <code>SymbolData</code>, which you set on the builder. This page follows
    one instance around that loop, then the three objects that run it, then
    the concrete symbol classes the library ships.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    Think of a symbol as a stage actor and the id as the costume. The theatre
    keeps a few actors per costume in the wings, and when the script calls
    for a cherry one of them walks on. The one rule for an actor is that it
    must never assume this is its first scene: every hook can fire more than
    once on the same object, so each one has to leave the object in a clean,
    known state.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The lifecycle</h2>

  <p>
    The base class is abstract, lives in <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/symbols/ReelSymbol.ts</code>, and implements <code>Disposable</code>. Everything it shows is a child of one public
    container, its <code>view</code>. Figure 1 is the loop one instance
    travels. Notice that the pool is keyed by symbol id: a recycled cherry
    only ever comes back as a cherry. Notice too that there is one way in
    (the registry creates it) and one way out (<code>destroy()</code>, when
    the pool is full or the set is torn down); everything else is a trip
    around the ring.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={540} caption="One instance, one identity, many trips around the ring. The registry creates and activates in one go; the pool is keyed by symbol id, so a recycled 'cherry' only ever comes back as 'cherry'; destroy() is the only way out.">
    <Box x={20} y={30} w={180} h={60} title="create" kind="code" mono={false} sub="registry.create(id)" />
    <Box x={255} y={30} w={230} h={78} title="activate(id)" kind="primary" sub={['view reset to defaults', 'then onActivate(id)']} />
    <Box x={510} y={200} w={190} h={78} title="on the reel" kind="accent" mono={false} sub={['resize(w, h), reel hooks', 'playWin() / playDestroy()']} />
    <Box x={20} y={200} w={180} h={78} title="pooled" kind="muted" mono={false} sub={["symbolId is ''", 'LIFO stack per id']} />
    <Box x={255} y={360} w={230} h={78} title="deactivate()" kind="primary" sub={['stopAnimation(); onDeactivate()', "id = '', view hidden + reset"]} />
    <Box x={510} y={460} w={190} h={60} title="destroy()" kind="code" sub="pool full, or teardown" />

    <Edge from={[200, 60]} to={[255, 60]} />
    <Edge from={[485, 69]} via={[[605, 69]]} to={[605, 200]} label="installed on the strip" labelDx={-105} labelDy={19.5} />
    <Edge from={[605, 278]} via={[[605, 399]]} to={[485, 399]} label="SymbolFactory.release()" labelDx={-109} labelDy={-8.5} />
    <Edge from={[255, 399]} via={[[110, 399]]} to={[110, 278]} label="pool.release(id)" labelDx={-10.5} labelDy={30} />
    <Edge from={[110, 200]} via={[[110, 150], [330, 150]]} to={[330, 108]} label="hit: pop + reset()" labelDy={-18} />
    <Edge from={[330, 438]} via={[[330, 490]]} to={[510, 490]} label="past maxPerKey" style="dashed" labelDx={-10} labelDy={-28} />
  </Diagram>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'Template methods', note: 'base class; they call your hooks' }, { kind: 'accent', label: 'Reel-driven hooks', note: 'all no-ops by default, all idempotent' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'Pooled', note: 'idle, invisible, id cleared' }, { kind: 'subtle', label: 'Allocation', note: 'dark boxes construct or dispose' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    The two primary boxes are template methods: the base class owns them,
    and they do the housekeeping before and after calling the hook you
    write. Activation stores the id, makes the view visible, and resets every
    property a previous life might have changed (alpha, scale, rotation,
    filters, tint and draw order) before calling your <code>onActivate()</code>. Deactivation does the mirror image: it stops
    any animation, calls your <code>onDeactivate()</code>, clears the id and
    the landing promise, hides the view and resets the same properties again.
    The tint is cleared on both sides, in the source's words, "so neither half
    has to trust the other".
  </p>

  <p>
    Two shortcuts are worth knowing. The pool's reset hook is <code>deactivate()</code>, so a recycled symbol is actually deactivated
    twice, once when it is released and again on the way out of the pool;
    that is harmless precisely because the hook is idempotent. And when a
    symbol wraps around and the next identity is the same id it already has,
    the reel skips the whole ring: it calls <code>stopAnimation()</code> and
    nothing else, with no trip through the pool. The table has the exact
    sequence of each template method.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>{['Template method', 'What it does, in order'].map((h) => <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['activate(symbolId)', 'stores the id; sets view.visible = true, alpha = 1, scale = (1, 1), rotation = 0, filters = null, tint = 0xffffff, zIndex = 0; then calls onActivate(symbolId)'],
          ['deactivate()', "calls stopAnimation(), then onDeactivate(); clears the id to '' and landing to null; hides the view and resets the same properties"],
          ['reset()', 'the pool hook; calls deactivate()'],
          ['destroy()', 'returns early if already destroyed; runs stopAnimation() and onDeactivate() if the view is still alive (a game can destroy a container that holds it); then onDestroy() and view.destroy({ children: true })'],
          ['same-id wrap', 'stopAnimation() only; no deactivate, no activate, no pool'],
        ].map(([m, d]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">{m}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80">{d}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What a subclass implements</h2>

  <p>
    Five members are required, and the base class provides a no-op or a
    sensible default for everything else. The optional hooks fall into two
    groups: presentation one-shots the game calls (landing, destroy, out and
    in), and reel-driven hooks the engine calls as the reel changes state, so
    a symbol can relax or sharpen its art as it spins, teases and lands.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>{['Member', 'Required', 'Default and contract'].map((h) => <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['onActivate(symbolId)', 'yes', 'swap the texture, restart idle, leave any ended pose'],
          ['onDeactivate()', 'yes', 'pause animations, clear listeners, leave a clean state'],
          ['playWin(): Promise', 'yes', 'resolves when the one-shot finishes; settle it on recycle or await hangs'],
          ['stopAnimation()', 'yes', 'return to idle immediately'],
          ['resize(width, height)', 'yes', 'store the size and reposition children. Runs on every symbol swap. Forgetting it is "the single most common why do my symbols scatter bug"'],
          ['onDestroy()', 'no', 'no-op; release subclass resources here'],
          ['playLanding()', 'no', 'resolves immediately; never called by the engine, for callers that land cells one at a time (Hold & Win lockAnimation)'],
          ['playDestroy({ delay, signal })', 'no', 'the cascade poof: pivot to the bounds centre (position compensated by (c - pivot) * scale), pop to 1.1 over 0.06 s with back.out(2.5), then scale 0 and alpha 0 over 0.14 s with power3.in starting 0.04 s in; about 200 ms. Abort skips to the end. Pivot, position and scale 1 are restored; alpha stays 0'],
          ['playOut(opts) / playIn(opts)', 'no', 'the mystery-reveal seam. Out: scale 0.72 and alpha 0 over 0.18 s, power2.in. In: owns its start pose (visible, alpha 0, scale 0.72), then scale 1 over 0.2 s with back.out(1.7) and alpha 1 over 0.16 s with power2.out. Scale always returns to 1'],
          ['cellInset', 'no', 'null, meaning the art fills the whole cell; sprite subclasses derive it from the texture trim'],
          ['applyCellQuad(quad | null)', 'no', 'curved reels: an affine contain-fit about the quad centre, paid for through the pivot. Must never move view.position'],
          ['onReelSpinStart(joinedMidSpin?)', 'no', 'fires on every strip symbol, buffers included, when the reel enters spin; again with true for each symbol installed mid-spin'],
          ['onReelSpinEnd()', 'no', 'fires just before the bounce'],
          ['onReelAnticipationStart()', 'no', 'fires when the tease begins; relax spin presentation so the strip is readable'],
          ['onReelLanded(ctx)', 'no', "fires before the reel's landing event; ctx = { reelIndex, reelCount, cell, visibleCells, symbolId }"],
        ].map(([m, r, d]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">{m}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{r}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80">{d}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Three more things the base class gives you. The first is <code>this.gsap</code>, a protected getter the factory binds when the
    symbol is created. Always animate through it rather than through an
    imported gsap, because a symlinked workspace can load two copies of the
    library.
    The second is <code>mainAxis</code> (<code>'x'</code> or <code>'y'</code>),
    bound the same way; only motion-blur effects read it. The third is <code>landing</code>, the promise started by the latest <code>onReelLanded()</code>. A subclass sets it through <code>trackLanding(run)</code>, the reel clears it when it leaves rest,
    and a board can <code>await symbol.landing</code> to know when a cell has
    finished arriving.
  </p>

  <h2>Registry, factory, pool</h2>

  <p>
    Three small objects run the loop, and it helps to separate their jobs.
    The <strong>registry</strong> knows which class draws which id. The <strong>pool</strong> remembers idle instances. The <strong>factory</strong> sits between the reel and the other two: a reel asks it for a symbol by
    id, and it either pops one from the pool or asks the registry to make a
    fresh one. Figure 2 follows one request left to right and the return trip
    underneath. Notice that the registry is only consulted on a miss, and
    that the dashed arrow is the only place a new object is allocated.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={360} caption="acquire() flows left to right and release() comes back underneath. The registry creates and activates; the factory binds gsap and the axis; the pool only remembers idle instances.">
    <Box x={20} y={70} w={180} h={78} title="Reel" sub={['resize, place, zIndex', 'emits symbol:created']} />
    <Box x={270} y={70} w={200} h={78} title="SymbolFactory" kind="accent" sub={['acquire(id) / release()', 'binds gsap and the axis']} />
    <Box x={530} y={70} w={170} h={78} title="ObjectPool" kind="code" sub={['Map<id, T[]>, LIFO', 'hit: pop + reset()']} />
    <Box x={530} y={230} w={170} h={78} title="SymbolRegistry" sub={['register(id, Class)', 'create: new + activate']} />

    <Edge from={[200, 109]} to={[270, 109]} label="acquire(id)" labelDy={-63} />
    <Edge from={[470, 109]} to={[530, 109]} label="pool.acquire(id)" labelDy={-63} />
    <Edge from={[615, 148]} to={[615, 230]} label="miss: create(id)" style="dashed" labelDx={-94} />
    <Edge from={[110, 148]} via={[[110, 300], [370, 300]]} to={[370, 148]} label="release(symbol)" labelDy={28} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    You meet the <strong>registry</strong> once, inside <code>builder.symbols(registry =&gt; ...)</code>, where you register each
    id with the class that draws it and that class's options. One entry per
    id, and a duplicate throws; if one class draws ten ids, that is ten
    calls. When asked to create an id, the registry constructs the class and
    activates the instance immediately, so a fresh symbol arrives already
    dressed; an unknown id throws with the list of registered ones. The
    registry has no idea of size, weight or draw order, which belong to <code>SymbolData</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>factory</strong> has been internal since PR #140; you never
    construct it, the builder does. When the pool misses, the factory asks
    the registry for a new instance and binds two things to it for life: the
    gsap instance and the main axis. They are bound at creation rather than
    on every acquire because a pooled symbol never moves between reel sets.
    On acquire, the factory only re-activates a symbol whose current id
    differs from the one requested, since a fresh instance was already
    activated by the registry. On release it deactivates the symbol, detaches
    it from any render layer it was promoted to, removes it from its parent,
    and hands it to the pool, which keeps it or, if that id's stack is already
    full, destroys it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>pool</strong>, <code>ObjectPool&lt;T&gt;</code> in <code>pool/ObjectPool.ts</code>, is a public export and is deliberately
    simple: one last-in-first-out stack per key. Acquiring pops and resets,
    or calls the factory on a miss. Releasing pushes, unless the item is
    already in the pool (a guard against the same object being handed out
    twice) or the stack is at its cap, in which case the item is destroyed.
    Releasing after the pool is destroyed is a no-op, while acquiring after
    that throws, because it means a ticker or promise is outliving teardown.
    There is no pre-allocation and no cap on live objects, only on how many
    idle ones are kept; <code>setMaxPerKey</code> trims any excess at once.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    Because the pool is keyed by id, an instance never changes identity while
    it is in reel use: a cherry stays a cherry for its whole life. That is why <code>CardSymbol</code> can get away with a no-op <code>onActivate</code>,
    and why <code>SpineReelSymbol</code>'s per-id map of Spine objects only
    ever holds one entry. The only identity swaps in the codebase happen on
    the <code>prewarmSpinTextures</code> scratch symbol and inside <code>StaticSpinSymbol</code>.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    The names, for reference. The factory is constructed as <code>SymbolFactory(registry, maxPoolPerKey = 20, gsap = DEFAULT_GSAP, mainAxis = 'y')</code>;
    its verbs are <code>acquire(id)</code> and <code>release(symbol)</code>,
    and release runs <code>deactivate()</code>, <code>view.parentRenderLayer?.detach(view)</code>, <code>view.removeFromParent()</code> and <code>pool.release(id, symbol)</code> in that order. The pool's verbs are <code>acquire(key)</code>, <code>release(key, item)</code>, <code>setMaxPerKey</code> and <code>destroy()</code>. The builder sizes the pool like this:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`// pool capacity, set by the builder
poolCapacity = builder.poolCapacity(n) ?? max(20, sum over reels of (visible + bufferStart + bufferEnd))
// a MultiWays reel counts at maxCells; the worst case for one id is the whole strip showing it
// DEFAULTS.maxPoolPerKey = 20; ReelSet.addReels() / removeReels() call setCapacityPerKey`}</code></pre>

  <h2>symbolData and draw order</h2>

  <p>
    Everything the engine knows about a symbol that is not how to draw it
    lives in <code>SymbolData</code> (<code>config/types.ts</code>): its <code>weight</code> in random draws, its draw order within the reel, whether
    it may escape the mask, and its size in cells. You set these on the
    builder, through <code>weights()</code> and <code>symbolData()</code>, and
    the builder fills in a weight of 10 and a draw order of 1 for every id
    you did not mention; the size defaults to one cell. A symbol marked <code>unmask: true</code> is lifted into the viewport's unmasked layer
    while its reel is at rest, so its art can overhang the cell.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`view.zIndex = symbolData.zIndex * 100 + stackingIndex
// stackingIndex = strip array index (cellStacking 'ascending') or length - 1 - index ('descending')
// Z_INDEX_BUDGET.symbolLayer = 100; pin overlays sit at 10000; destroy and swap bumps use 1000`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    That formula is how the engine turns the metadata into a PixiJS zIndex:
    your draw order, times a hundred, plus the symbol's position in the
    strip, so cells of the same kind layer in strip order. It only sorts
    within one reel's container; to draw across reels you need <code>unmask: true</code>. If you need a different rule, pass a resolver
    to <code>builder.symbolZIndex()</code>; it replaces the formula outright.
    The resolver receives <code>{'{ symbolId, symbolData, reelIndex, reelCount, arrayIndex, visibleCell | null, visibleCells, atRest, defaultZIndex }'}</code> and must be pure and cheap, because the engine calls it after every
    wrap, snap, swap and rest transition. One drift to know: the ReelSet doc
    table says the default draw order is <code>?? 0</code>, but the builder
    writes <code>?? 1</code>, so built symbols start at <code>100 + index</code>.
    The code is the source of truth here.
  </p>

  <h2>Big symbols</h2>

  <p>
    Some symbols are bigger than one cell: a 2 x 2 bonus tile, a wild that
    fills a whole reel. The engine handles these without a special symbol
    class. The server names the symbol in one cell only, the <em>anchor</em>, and the other cells the block covers become invisible
    placeholders. Figure 3 shows a 2 x 2 block anchored at reel 1, cell 0.
    Notice that the anchor's view has been resized to cover all four cells,
    gap included, and that the three other cells are stubs rather than
    cherries or blanks.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={330} caption="A 2 x 2 block anchored at reel 1, cell 0. The server sends 'bonus' at the anchor only; the other three cells become invisible OCCUPIED stubs, and the anchor's view is resized to cover the whole block, gap included.">
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((r) => [0, 1, 2].map((c) => {
      if (r === 1 && c === 0) return null;
      return <Cell x={cx(r)} y={cy(c)} size={CS} label={inBlock(r, c) ? 'stub' : `${r},${c}`} sub={inBlock(r, c) ? 'OCCUPIED' : undefined} kind={inBlock(r, c) ? 'empty' : 'plain'} />;
    }))}
    <Cell x={cx(1)} y={cy(0)} size={CS} label="anchor" sub="'bonus'" kind="hot" />
    <rect x={cx(1) - 4} y={cy(0) - 4} width={2 * CS + GAP + 8} height={2 * CS + GAP + 8} rx="10" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 4 3" />
    <Edge from={[cx(1), 60]} to={[cx(2) + CS, 60]} arrow="both" label="size.reels = 2" labelDy={-30} />
    <Edge from={[cx(4) + CS + 20, cy(0)]} to={[cx(4) + CS + 20, cy(1) + CS]} arrow="both" label="size.cells = 2" labelDx={86} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    You declare a block on the builder, not in the registry: <code>symbolData({'{ bonus: { size: { reels: 2, cells: 2 }, zIndex: 5 } }'})</code>.
    Its weight must be 0, because random fill never places blocks; only the
    server does, and only at anchor cells. <code>size.reels</code> always
    runs along the cross axis and <code>size.cells</code> along the main
    axis, whatever the orientation. The anchor's view is resized to the block
    size, which here is two cells plus one gap on each axis, and <code>getVisibleGrid()</code> reports <code>'bonus'</code> in all four
    cells, so code that reads the grid sees the block, not the stubs.
  </p>

  <p>
    The function that lays blocks out is <code>coordinateBigSymbols</code> in <code>spin/bigSymbolBlocks.ts</code>. It is pure, it runs in the spin
    controller before any reel builds its frame, it throws with a named
    message when a block does not fit, and it sits on every path that puts a
    grid on the reels: landing, the initial frame, adding or removing reels
    and rows, and pinning or moving pins. A block may be partly off screen:
    an anchor can sit in the start buffer with its tail visible, and stubs
    can spill into the end buffer. The stub itself, <code>OccupiedStub</code>,
    is an internal symbol in <code>core/Reel.ts</code> that sets itself
    invisible on activate; each reel allocates its own stubs lazily and
    reuses them rather than going through the factory.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three limits: big symbols cannot be combined with <code>multiways()</code>,
    a non-anchor cell cannot be pinned, and a block spanning reels whose cell
    sizes differ logs a one-time warning. The recipes use <code>zIndex: 5</code> for anchors so a block draws above its neighbours. See <a href="/guides/big-symbols/">the big symbols guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The concrete classes</h2>

  <p>
    The library ships a symbol class for each common kind of art, plus two
    for debugging and two internal helpers. The table has each one's options
    with their defaults and what it does with the hooks above; most games
    start with <code>SpriteSymbol</code> and reach for a Spine class when the
    art is rigged.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>{['Class', 'Options and defaults', 'Behaviour'].map((h) => <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['SpriteSymbol', 'textures: Record<id, Texture>; anchor, in code { 0, 0 }', 'onActivate swaps the texture and syncs the perspective mesh. playWin: scale pulse to 1.15 over 0.15 s, yoyo, one repeat, power2.inOut; _killWinTween always settles a pending await. resize sets sprite width and height only. On curved reels it draws through a PerspectiveMesh and falls back to the affine fit for atlas sub-frames; cellInset comes from the texture trim.'],
          ['AnimatedSpriteSymbol', 'frames: Record<id, Texture[]>; animationSpeed 1; anchor, in code { 0, 0 }; loop = false', 'onActivate sets textures and gotoAndStop(0). playWin is gotoAndPlay(0), returning to frame 0 on complete. onFrameChange pushes every frame to the perspective mesh so a curved win does not freeze. resize sets the size and positions the sprite at size * anchor.'],
          ['SpineSymbol (core, optional peer)', "skeletonDataMap; idleAnimation 'idle'; winAnimation 'win'; defaultSkin 'default'; strict false", 'Loaded by a dynamic import; constructing before whenSpineReady() resolves throws. One Spine per instance, rebuilt on a new id, idle looped on track 0. playWin plays win once; listener on the TrackEntry. onDeactivate settles the win and clearTracks() only. resize is a non-uniform stretch from getBounds() with no positioning; from reading the code, a second resize at the same size can undo the fit because getBounds() includes the current scale.'],
          ['SpineReelSymbol (pixi-reels/spine)', "spineMap { skeleton, atlas, skin? }; idle 'idle', win 'win', landing 'landing', out 'disintegration', blur 'blur'; animations overrides per id; scale 1; autoPlayBlur false; autoPlayLanding false | fn", 'One Spine per id, parked (visible false, autoUpdate false) when not on screen, update(0) on activate so a snapshot sees the pose. playOneShot(name, { track = 0, then }) returns a promise settled on complete, interrupt or end; then defaults to idle on track 0 and clear elsewhere, hold keeps an overlay. playWin, playLanding (via trackLanding) and playOut (then: hold) are built on it. playDestroy plays the out animation or falls back to the base poof. playBlur is idempotent. onDeactivate settles one-shots, clearTracks, setToSetupPose, parks. resize re-centres only: no fit-scaling, author at cell size or use scale.'],
          ['CardSymbol (debug)', 'color; label; textColor 0xffffff; font Roboto Condensed 32 bold', 'identity fixed at construction, so onActivate and onDeactivate are no-ops. playWin: glyph to 1.4 over 0.18 s with back.out(2), a rotation wobble of 0.12 rad, back to 1 at 0.32 s, fill flashes 0xffe168 at 0.06 s and restores at 0.42 s. resize draws a (0, 0, w, h) rect with an inset 2 px stroke at alpha 0.25 and sets fontSize = max(7, floor(min(0.38 * h, 0.7 * w / (len * 0.45)))). Exports CARD_DECK (7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A) and WILD_CARD.'],
          ['PhaseCardSymbol (debug)', 'label; textColor; colors; landedMs 350', "extends CardSymbol. setPhase(phase) repaints; 'landed' reverts to 'rest' after landedMs. static watch(reels) subscribes to phase:enter, landed and symbol:created and returns an unsubscribe."],
          ['EmptySymbol', 'none', 'every hook is a no-op; renders nothing. Hold & Win blanks, cascade holes, placeholders. HoldAndWinBuilder registers it under emptyId.'],
          ['OccupiedStub (internal)', 'none', 'invisible filler for non-anchor big-symbol cells; allocated by the Reel, not the factory.'],
          ['StaticSpinSymbol + SpinTextureCache', "createInner; cache; spinTexture 'blurred'; blurRampMs 120; blur (axis = the set's mainAxis)", 'Wraps an inner symbol. On spin start it captures a crisp snapshot while the inner is still live, crossfades to a pre-baked blur over 120 ms, then deactivates the inner so it costs nothing while spinning. Anticipation fades the blur out. On spin end it reactivates the inner on the final id. The cache is one per reel set, keyed by id plus size (rounded to 0.001 px) plus axis; blur strength defaults to 0.2 of the cell, quality 4, padding ceil(strength), baked once with no per-frame filter. prewarmSpinTextures() fills it at load time.'],
        ].map(([c, o, b]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">{c}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80 text-xs">{o}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80">{b}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The Spine animation vocabulary</h2>

  <p>
    A Spine symbol is driven by animation names, and the library agrees on a
    small vocabulary so a rigged symbol works out of the box: one looping
    idle, a few one-shots for the moments a slot cares about, and a looping
    blur for fast spins. Every name can be overridden per id. The table lists
    each role, its default name, and what triggers it.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>{['Role', 'Default name', 'Loops', 'How it plays'].map((h) => <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['Resting', 'idle', 'yes', 'automatically on activate'],
          ['Reel-stop one-shot', 'landing', 'no', 'playLanding() or autoPlayLanding'],
          ['Win', 'win', 'no', 'playWin()'],
          ['Cascade pop / exit', 'disintegration', 'no', 'playOut() / playDestroy()'],
          ['Fast spin', 'blur', 'yes', 'playBlur() or autoPlayBlur'],
          ['Neighbour cue', 'reactions/react_{u,d,l,r,ul,ur,dl,dr}', 'no', 'raw spine.state, or playOneShot(name, { track: 1 })'],
        ].map((row) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border">
            {row.map((v, i) => <td class:list={['px-4 py-2', i === 1 ? 'font-mono text-xs' : 'text-foreground/80']}>{v}</td>)}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A missing animation is a silent no-op. The library never throws because
    a skeleton lacks a name; the one opt-in exception is the core <code>SpineSymbol</code>'s <code>strict</code> mode (ADR 011). There is
    one rule about listeners that comes from a real bug. Attach completion
    listeners to the <code>TrackEntry</code>, never to the whole animation
    state, and null the listener once it has fired, because Spine pools track
    entries. Never clear the state's listeners during the symbol's life
    either, since the game may have added some of its own; <code>clearListeners()</code> runs only in <code>onDestroy</code>.
    Removing a state listener in the middle of a dispatch once made the other
    listeners miss their events. The Spine runtime stays out of the core
    bundle: import <code>SpineReelSymbol</code> only from <code>pixi-reels/spine</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For completeness, <code>PHASE_CARD_COLORS</code> is the palette <code>PhaseCardSymbol</code> paints with: rest <code>0x4b5563</code>,
    start <code>0x38bdf8</code>, spin <code>0x2563eb</code>, anticipation <code>0xf59e0b</code>, stop <code>0x8b5cf6</code>,
    landed <code>0x22c55e</code>, adjust <code>0xec4899</code>, cascade:fall <code>0x0ea5e9</code>, cascade:place <code>0x14b8a6</code>, cascade:dropIn <code>0x06b6d4</code>, other <code>0x9ca3af</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Random-draw pools and scopes</h2>

  <p>
    The word "pool" is used for a second, unrelated thing: the set of ids a
    reel may draw from at random while it spins, and how likely each one is.
    A <code>SymbolPool</code> is <code>{'{ weights?, exclude? }'}</code>, and
    a weight of 0 removes an id as fully as excluding it. A <em>scope</em> says where the pool applies: optionally one reel, and one of four slot
    kinds. <code>'spinning'</code> is every random draw and the default. <code>'buffer'</code> is both off-window sides. <code>'bufferStart'</code> is the side with the smaller coordinate (above on a vertical set, left on
    a horizontal one) and <code>'bufferEnd'</code> the other. Pools layer,
    widest first:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`base weights -> global spinning -> per-reel spinning -> global buffer -> per-reel buffer -> global side -> per-reel side
// weights override per id; exclusions accumulate: a narrower layer can only ban more, never re-admit
// changes apply to the next draw; symbols already on the strip stay until they wrap out`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    At build time pools come from <code>builder.randomSymbols(pool, scope)</code>;
    at runtime <code>reelSet.randomSymbols</code> exposes <code>set(pool | null, scope?)</code>, <code>clear()</code> and <code>weights(scope?)</code>. The provider validates every id, compiles
    every reachable table, and rolls the change back with the error <code>No symbol left to draw for ... cells on reel N</code> if a scope
    would end up empty. Each draw is a binary search over a cached
    cumulative-weight table using the injected <code>rng</code> (<code>builder.rng(fn)</code>, default <code>Math.random</code>), so a
    weighted draw stays cheap. See <a href="/architecture/frame-pipeline/">the frame pipeline</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Conventions and gotchas</h2>

  <p>
    Most symbol bugs come from one of five habits, and most of those follow
    from pooling. First, the view's origin is the cell's top-left corner, and the
    reel owns it: the reel places <code>view</code> there and reads <code>view.position</code> back to find the slot, so a symbol must never
    move its own position. Curve fits and destroy tweens use the pivot
    instead. Second, implement <code>resize()</code> properly: it runs on
    every symbol swap, and it must store the size and reposition children
    without assuming an anchor centres anything.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    The option docs for <code>SpriteSymbol</code> and <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol</code> say the anchor defaults to 0.5, but the
    code defaults to 0,0, and <code>SpriteSymbol.resize</code> does not
    compensate for an anchor. The code is the source of truth here; anchor
    0,0 is the safe convention, and a symbol that scatters across its cell
    has almost always got this or <code>resize()</code> wrong.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Third, settle every promise when a symbol is recycled. Each built-in does
    this in its own way (<code>_killWinTween</code> in SpriteSymbol, <code>stopAnimation</code> in AnimatedSpriteSymbol, <code>_settleWin</code> and <code>_settleOneShots</code> in the Spine classes, <code>_endWin</code> in CardSymbol), and if yours does not, an <code>await playWin()</code> somewhere in the game hangs forever. Fourth, remember the same-id fast
    path: it skips deactivate and activate entirely, so the reel calls <code>stopAnimation()</code> itself and resets alpha, scale, rotation,
    filters and tint, and no <code>symbol:created</code> event fires. Fifth,
    for Spine, parked skeletons do not tick. Only the on-screen Spine has <code>autoUpdate</code>, which is what makes a Hold &amp; Win board with
    hundreds of cells affordable, but it means you must reset to the setup
    pose on deactivate or a disintegrated symbol comes back invisible.
  </p>

  <p>
    All of this runs without a renderer. <code>HeadlessSymbol</code> from <code>pixi-reels/testing</code> is a <code>ReelSymbol</code> that draws
    nothing, and the whole lifecycle above, pool included, runs in Node.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this lives</h2>

  <p>
    Under <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/</code>: <code>symbols/ReelSymbol.ts</code> and the <code>Sprite</code>, <code>AnimatedSprite</code>, <code>Spine</code>, <code>Card</code>, <code>PhaseCard</code> and <code>Empty</code> files beside it, with <code>SymbolRegistry.ts</code>, <code>SymbolFactory.ts</code> and <code>PerspectiveCell.ts</code>; <code>pool/ObjectPool.ts</code>; <code>spine/SpineReelSymbol.ts</code>; <code>snapshot/SpinTextureCache.ts</code> and <code>StaticSpinSymbol.ts</code>; <code>frame/SymbolPool.ts</code> and <code>RandomSymbolProvider.ts</code>; <code>spin/bigSymbolBlocks.ts</code>; and the <code>OccupiedStub</code> in <code>core/Reel.ts</code>.
    Guides: <a href="/guides/symbols/">symbols</a>, <a href="/guides/win-animations/">win animations</a>, <a href="/guides/big-symbols/">big symbols</a>. Recipes: <a href="/recipes/symbols/">symbols</a>, <a href="/recipes/big-symbols/">big symbols</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="remember">
    A symbol is borrowed, not made. Every hook can run again on the same
    object, so each one must leave a clean state; <code>resize()</code> runs
    on every swap and must position everything; and the view's position
    belongs to the reel. Get those three right and the pool, the registry and
    the factory take care of the rest.
  </Callout>
</Architecture>
